/T (Reforming International Fiscal Rules and IMF Debt Sustainability Policies to Strengthen Economic Resilience During Periods of Volatility)
/E (Reforming International Fiscal Rules and IMF Debt Sustainability Policies to Strengthen Economic Resilience During Periods of Volatility)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
485 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 259 0 R
/T (The IMF's Debt Sustainability Framework is the main tool multilateral institutions use to judge how much a country can safely borrow, but it's faced growing criticism for relying on rigid thresholds, overly optimistic growth assumptions, and a pretty narrow definition of what counts as "sustainable" debt. Reform proposals under discussion range from incorporating climate and disaster risk into sustainability assessments, to separating technical economic analysis more clearly from political judgment, to creating faster, more predictable procedures for countries approaching debt distress. Delegates should think about how fiscal rules can be made responsive to shocks, pandemics, commodity price swings, and conflict, without abandoning the discipline that keeps global capital markets stable.)
/E (The IMF's Debt Sustainability Framework is the main tool multilateral institutions use to judge how much a country can safely borrow, but it's faced growing criticism for relying on rigid thresholds, overly optimistic growth assumptions, and a pretty narrow definition of what counts as "sustainable" debt. Reform proposals under discussion range from incorporating climate and disaster risk into sustainability assessments, to separating technical economic analysis more clearly from political judgment, to creating faster, more predictable procedures for countries approaching debt distress. Delegates should think about how fiscal rules can be made responsive to shocks, pandemics, commodity price swings, and conflict, without abandoning the discipline that keeps global capital markets stable.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
486 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 259 0 R
/T (Financing Strategic Investments to Reduce Economic Vulnerabilities in an Uncertain Global Environment)
/E (Financing Strategic Investments to Reduce Economic Vulnerabilities in an Uncertain Global Environment)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
487 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 259 0 R
/T (Even fiscally disciplined governments need channels to invest in the infrastructure, education, and climate resilience that reduce long-term vulnerability. This subtopic asks delegates to weigh instruments like expanded multilateral development bank lending, blended finance structures that use a small amount of public money to unlock much larger private investment, green and sustainability-linked bonds, and reallocated Special Drawing Rights. The core challenge is designing financing mechanisms that actually reach the countries with the least fiscal space, instead of concentrating new capital in economies that can already borrow cheaply on their own.)
/E (Even fiscally disciplined governments need channels to invest in the infrastructure, education, and climate resilience that reduce long-term vulnerability. This subtopic asks delegates to weigh instruments like expanded multilateral development bank lending, blended finance structures that use a small amount of public money to unlock much larger private investment, green and sustainability-linked bonds, and reallocated Special Drawing Rights. The core challenge is designing financing mechanisms that actually reach the countries with the least fiscal space, instead of concentrating new capital in economies that can already borrow cheaply on their own.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
