2280 0 obj
<<
/Title (The IMF's Debt Sustainability Framework is the main tool multilateral institutions use to judge how much a country can safely borrow, but it's faced growing criticism for relying on rigid thresholds, overly optimistic growth assumptions, and a pretty narrow definition of what counts as "sustainable" debt. Reform proposals under discussion range from incorporating climate and disaster risk into sustainability assessments, to separating technical economic analysis more clearly from political judgment, to creating faster, more predictable procedures for countries approaching debt distress. Delegates should think about how fiscal rules can be made responsive to shocks, pandemics, commodity price swings, and conflict, without abandoning the discipline that keeps global capital markets stable.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2265 0 R
>>
endobj
2281 0 obj
<<
/Title (Even fiscally disciplined governments need channels to invest in the infrastructure, education, and climate resilience that reduce long-term vulnerability. This subtopic asks delegates to weigh instruments like expanded multilateral development bank lending, blended finance structures that use a small amount of public money to unlock much larger private investment, green and sustainability-linked bonds, and reallocated Special Drawing Rights. The core challenge is designing financing mechanisms that actually reach the countries with the least fiscal space, instead of concentrating new capital in economies that can already borrow cheaply on their own.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2266 0 R
/First 2290 0 R
/Last 2290 0 R
/Count -1
>>
endobj
